Q: Deleting a Standard TCP IP port using xcvdata not working A: <p>Win11 22631.6494</p>
<p>VS:18.2.1</p>
<p>Reference:</p>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/ddi/tcpxcv/ns-tcpxcv-_delete_port_data_1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/ddi/tcpxcv/ns-tcpxcv-_delete_port_data_1</a></p>
<p>For me the &quot;solution&quot; was this:</p>
<pre><code>bool DeletePortXcv(const std::wstring &amp;portName) {
    HANDLE hPrinter = NULL;

    PRINTER_DEFAULTS PrinterDefaults;
    memset(&amp;PrinterDefaults, 0, sizeof(PrinterDefaults));

    PrinterDefaults.pDatatype = NULL;
    PrinterDefaults.pDevMode = NULL;
    //SERVER_ACCESS_ADMINISTER !!!
    PrinterDefaults.DesiredAccess = SERVER_ACCESS_ADMINISTER;

    DWORD errorCode = NULL;

    LPSTR messageBuffer = nullptr;

    size_t size = 0;

    /* same as create monitor*/
    wchar_t monitorName[] = L&quot;,XcvMonitor Standard TCP/IP Port&quot;;
    if(!OpenPrinter(monitorName, &amp;hPrinter, &amp;PrinterDefaults)){
        errorCode = GetLastError();

        messageBuffer = nullptr;

        /* format error*/
        size = FormatMessageA(FORMAT_MESSAGE_ALLOCATE_BUFFER | FORMAT_MESSAGE_FROM_SYSTEM | FORMAT_MESSAGE_IGNORE_INSERTS, NULL, errorCode, MAKELANGID(LANG_NEUTRAL, SUBLANG_DEFAULT), (LPSTR)&amp;messageBuffer, 0, NULL);

        if(size &gt; 0){
            std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Error at OpenPrinterW (monitor open) (&quot; &lt;&lt; errorCode &lt;&lt; &quot;): &quot; &lt;&lt; messageBuffer &lt;&lt; std::endl;
        }
        return false;
    }


    DELETE_PORT_DATA_1 pd; /*USE DELETE_PORT_DATA_1 insted of PORT_DATA_1, DO NOT USE:PORT_DATA_1*/
    memset(&amp;pd, 0, sizeof(pd));

    pd.dwVersion = 1;                 /*MUST: 1*/

    wcscpy_s(pd.psztPortName, MAX_PORTNAME_LEN, portName.c_str());

    DWORD dwStatus = 0;
    DWORD cbOutputNeeded = 0;
    DWORD dwXcvResult = 0;
    /*_DELETE_PORT_DATA_1*/
    /* port name string*/
    /* use this:sizeof(DELETE_PORT_DATA_1)*/
    BOOL bRes = XcvData(
        hPrinter,
        L&quot;DeletePort&quot;,
        (BYTE*)&amp;pd,
        sizeof(DELETE_PORT_DATA_1),
        NULL,
        0,
        &amp;cbOutputNeeded,
        &amp;dwXcvResult
    );

    errorCode = GetLastError();

    messageBuffer = nullptr;

    /* error formating*/
    size = FormatMessageA(FORMAT_MESSAGE_ALLOCATE_BUFFER | FORMAT_MESSAGE_FROM_SYSTEM | FORMAT_MESSAGE_IGNORE_INSERTS, NULL, errorCode, MAKELANGID(LANG_NEUTRAL, SUBLANG_DEFAULT), (LPSTR)&amp;messageBuffer, 0, NULL);

    if(size &gt; 0){
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Error at XcvData (DeletePort) (&quot; &lt;&lt; errorCode &lt;&lt; &quot;): &quot; &lt;&lt; messageBuffer &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    }

    ClosePrinter(hPrinter);

    /* ERROR_SUCCESS*/
    return (bRes &amp;&amp; dwXcvResult == ERROR_SUCCESS);
}
</code></pre>

Q: C++20 with u8, char8_t and std::string A: <p>Another way to use u8 literals as <code>const char*</code>, would be a user-defined literal (see <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/user_literal" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/user_literal</a>):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::string operator &quot;&quot;_S(const char8_t* str, std::size_t) {
    return reinterpret_cast&lt; const char* &gt;(str);
}
const char* operator &quot;&quot;_C(const char8_t* str, std::size_t) {
    return reinterpret_cast&lt; const char* &gt;(str);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Usage:</strong>
Then it can be used like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::string myString = u8&quot;●&quot;_S;


SetConsoleOutputCP(CP_UTF8);
std::cout &lt;&lt; u8&quot;Привет, мир!&quot;_C &lt;&lt; std::endl;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>
<p>The code above defines two user-defined literals <code>u8&quot;…&quot;_S</code> and <code>u8&quot;…&quot;_C</code> (remember: the literal <code>u8&quot;…&quot;</code> in C++20 is of type <code>const char8_t*</code>). The <code>_S</code> literal created a <code>std::string</code> and the <code>_C</code> literal creates a <code>const char *</code>.</p>
<p>That means all literals of the form <code>u8&quot;…&quot;_C</code> can be used like <code>&quot;…&quot;</code> literals, while all literals of the form <code>u8&quot;…&quot;_S</code> can be used like <code>&quot;…&quot;s</code> literals.</p>
<p><strong>Update 2026-02-03</strong></p>
<p>I updated to code to use a leading underscore, as the <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/user_literal#Literal_operators" rel="nofollow noreferrer">cppreference</a> (or <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/usrlit.suffix" rel="nofollow noreferrer">16.4.5.3.6</a>) suggested. Thanks for @<a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/1969455/brandlingo">Brandlingo</a> for pointing that out in the comment.</p>
